<title>BELGIUM: EU, Japan car agreement for 1997 - details.</title>
<headline>EU, Japan car agreement for 1997 - details.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-03-26</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Commission and Japan have agreed on how many cars Japan can export in 1997 to the 15-nation European Union as well as to five member states which previously had national restrictions on Japanese car imports.</p>
<p>For the whole of the bloc, Japan will be able to ship 1,092,000 cars to the EU in 1997 compared with 1,093,000 units in 1996, a Commission statement said.</p>
<p>These are the export limits for the five countries:</p>
<p>				   1997	  1996</p>
<p>    France		    103,700	104,100</p>
<p>    Italy			88,100	 66,400</p>
<p>    Spain			51,800	 46,200</p>
<p>    Portugal		   33,200	 33,800</p>
<p>    United Kingdom	  179,900	181,100</p>
<p>The EU agreed in 1991 to progressively open up its market to Japanese cars, ending restrictions completely at the end of 1999.</p>
<p>The figures negotiated each year take into account market developments and also the preceding year's figures.</p>
<p>Over the last two years Japan has not managed to fill the whole quota. Last year, only 808,000 units were exported to the EU, about 75 percent of the agreed limit.</p>
<p>-- Brussels newsroom +32 2 287 68 11</p>
</text>
